          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"This is a direct first-person, present-tense avowal of love. The remainder of Goneril’s turn elaborates the asserted state as surpassingly strong, so reporting or presenting her love as true is the expression’s dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression claims a present degree of filial love but does not itself create, renew, or affirm an obligation to perform anything. Lear’s announced distribution of bounty supplies a consequential setting, not undertaking force for Goneril’s words; her declaration functions as an answer to his test rather than as a promise. Lear’s subsequent grant is uptake of that answer as satisfying the test, not acceptance of a commitment from Goneril.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within an extended, formally structured response to Lear’s command. Although emotionally intense and hyperbolic, it is not represented as a reflex-like or involuntary affective discharge beyond its avowing function.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression serves as a competitive and persuasive answer in Lear’s public test: Goneril presents herself as the daughter who loves him most and therefore merits the announced bounty. This is a contextual claim to standing or reward, without by itself proving manipulation or insincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public court ceremony, Lear asks his daughters to declare which loves him most and explicitly connects their answers to the division of his kingdom. Goneril answers first with an elaborate assertion that her love exceeds ordinary comparison; Lear then grants her and Albany a territorial inheritance.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal occurrence does not establish an undertaking by Goneril: it avows maximal present love without positioning her as bound to a specified or general future duty. Lear treats the declaration as a successful answer and grants territory, but that is uptake within his love test rather than acceptance of a promise. The supplied evidence does not settle Goneril’s sincerity or actual affection.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"This passage is recognised as the opening love-test scene of William Shakespeare’s King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied passages, not on remembered later events.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene establishes the public, competitive, and reward-linked setting but does not independently settle whether Goneril truly feels the love she avows, intends deception, or understands her words as carrying any unstated continuing obligation. Later consequences and conduct are not supplied and are not used.\"}"
        }
